<template>
  <div class="rag-story">
    <p class="back-link back-link-top"><router-link :to="{ path: '/projects', query: { to: 'pwcRag' } }"><span class="back-link-arrow" aria-hidden="true">←</span><span>Back to Builds</span></router-link></p>
    <h1 data-scroll-reveal>Building Reliable AI Answers from Complex Documents</h1>

    <article data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">
        This project began with a practical requirement. When someone asks an AI assistant about a public rule or
        service, the answer needs the complete condition, qualification, or decision path. A missing note or broken
        table can change the meaning of the answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Complex source documents made that difficult. Standard PDF reading tools could split a merged table, break a table or
        paragraph at a page boundary, detach a note from the sentence it qualified, or leave a flowchart outside the
        searchable text. An AI system could then find a passage that sounded relevant while missing the condition
        needed to interpret it correctly.
      </p>
      <p>
        I designed a document preparation workflow that repaired those relationships before the material reached the
        AI assistant. MinerU extracted structured text and tables. A custom linker reconnected notes with the sentences
        they qualified. Mermaid turned each flowchart into searchable text while preserving its decisions and branches.
        Technically, this forms a structure-preserving ingestion pipeline for retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG.
        The stages below follow one controlled test document through the full process.
      </p>

      <h2>Designing the recommendation around the client</h2>
      <p>
        The recommendation needed to work beyond the prototype. I compared possible designs against the client&rsquo;s
        existing infrastructure, operating cost, implementation effort, and the people who would run and maintain the
        system. The client also preferred an AI setup built around one model.
      </p>
      <p>
        I tested whether one visual-language model should read both tables and flowcharts. The proof of concept supported
        a leaner division of work. MinerU handled structured text and tables, one visual-language model handled visual
        material such as flowcharts, and explicit rules repaired cross-page references that needed predictable matching.
      </p>
      <p>
        The final recommendation brought together the requirements, cost and infrastructure analysis, proof-of-concept
        evidence, technical tradeoffs, and a practical path from prototype to implementation. The result was a complete
        consulting recommendation as well as a working technical design.
