        {
          key: 'notes',
          name: 'Connecting notes to their citations',
          body: 'Some sentences ended with a reference such as (4), while the corresponding Note (4) appeared several pages later. I wrote a rule-based script that located the matching note, repaired cases where the parser had split it, and copied it beside the sentence that cited it. The standalone copy was then removed so the document contained one complete version rather than two disconnected fragments.',
          rules: [
            'Detect a citation such as (4) at the end of a sentence.',
            'Locate the matching Note (4) or Remark (4) elsewhere in the relevant section.',
            'Repair cases where the parser has divided one note into multiple fragments.',
            'Normalise small inconsistencies in the parsed note labels.',
            'Copy the note beside the sentence that cites it, then remove the duplicated standalone copy.'
          ]
        },
        {
          key: 'mermaid',
          name: 'Making flowcharts searchable',
          body: 'Flowcharts contained useful decision logic that ordinary text search could not read reliably from an image. I represented each diagram in Mermaid, a text format that preserves its nodes, branches, and labels. The same source can be rendered back into a diagram for readers and searched as text by the AI system.'
        },
        {
          key: 'chunking',
          name: 'Creating answer-ready passages',
          body: 'RAGFlow divides the processed document into smaller passages, a process often called chunking. Repairing cross-page notes, tables, and paragraphs first allows each passage to carry a complete rule and the qualification needed to interpret it. Each passage can then support an answer on its own.'
        },
        {
          key: 'embedding',
          name: 'Matching questions by meaning',
          body: 'Each passage is converted into a numerical representation of its meaning, known as an embedding. A question receives the same treatment, allowing the system to find relevant material even when the wording differs. Mermaid diagrams enter this stage as text, so their decision logic can be found alongside paragraphs and tables.'
        },
        {
          key: 'retrieval',
          name: 'Finding the relevant evidence',
          body: 'The system compares the question with every prepared passage and returns the closest matches. In the test case, the passage containing both the sentence and its linked note becomes the top result. This confirms that the earlier repairs remain useful when the system searches for evidence.'
        },
        {
          key: 'generation',
          name: 'Writing the answer',
          body: 'The selected passages are given to DeepSeek V4-flash, which writes the answer from that material.'
        }
      ],
      research: [
        {
          title: 'Baidu Unlimited OCR',
          body: 'I evaluated Baidu Unlimited OCR because its multi-page mode appeared capable of handling relationships across pages. On difficult public government documents, it preserved page-level layout reasonably well. The cross-page links required for these questions still needed an explicit linking step. Its multi-page mode also used a lower-detail configuration, and the available description indicated that its training data joined individual pages together.'
        },
        {
          title: 'Visual and multimodal embedding',
          body: 'I also tested image-based and multimodal alternatives. Treating each page as an independent image left cross-page notes disconnected from the sentences that cited them. In the RAGFlow path I evaluated, a vision model first converted images to text before creating the meaning representation. Reconstructing the relationships explicitly in text produced the most controllable result for these documents.'
        },
        {
          title: 'Flowchart variants',
          body: 'I tested three ways to process each flowchart. OCR captured the visible text while losing edges and reading order. Free-form descriptions from a visual-language model varied from one figure to another. A structured prompt that produced Mermaid preserved every node, edge, and label and answered both test questions consistently.'
        },
        {
          title: 'Design findings',
          body: 'Three design decisions followed from the tests. Tables and paragraphs are repaired before they are divided into passages because later stages cannot recover structure that has already been split incorrectly. Repeated note numbers are matched using position and surrounding context. Most importantly, every resulting passage carries both the rule and the note that limits or explains it, giving the answer complete evidence.'
        }
      ]
    };
  },
};
</script>

